ChatSched — Claude fixes, Sept 1
=================================

Scope: this file documents ONLY the first task resolved from
"Claude To fix 1..txt" — item 5, CRITICAL: Admin role escalation risk.
Items 6 onward in that file (and everything in "Claude to fix 2.txt" /
"Claude to fix 3.txt") were left untouched, per instructions.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. What the finding actually said, verified against the real source
------------------------------------------------------------------------
The audit named two suspects and asked for a fix. I read the actual code
before writing anything, and found two genuinely separate, real holes —
not one:

  A) handle_new_user() (originally in schema.sql, redefined by
     schema_phase5.sql, redefined again by schema_phase7.sql) builds a
     new profiles row using:
         coalesce(new.raw_user_meta_data ->> 'role', 'business')
     It only supplies a fallback if the 'role' key is missing — it never
     validates the value that IS present. profiles_role_check (added in
     schema_phase5.sql) allows the value to be 'business', 'admin', or
     'publisher'. Register.tsx and AuthContext.tsx only ever send
     "business" or "publisher" in their TypeScript types, but that's a
     UI-layer constraint only — nothing stops a direct call to
     supabase.auth.signUp() with options.data = { role: 'admin' } (e.g.
     from curl, Postman, or a modified frontend build) from producing an
     admin account the instant the row is created.

  B) profiles_update_own_or_admin (schema.sql):
         for update using (auth.uid() = id or public.is_admin())
     has no WITH CHECK clause. Postgres reuses the USING clause as the
     check when none is given, so a logged-in business or publisher can
     run `update profiles set role = 'admin' where id = auth.uid()`
     today and it succeeds outright — RLS restricts which ROWS an update
     can touch, not which COLUMNS within an allowed row. The codebase
     already knew this pattern and had already fixed it once:
     prevent_self_verification() (schema_phase7.sql) resets
     email_verified/phone_verified/business_verified back to their old
     values whenever a real logged-in non-admin session tries to change
     them. role was simply never added to that same protection.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Files changed
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- supabase/schema_phase81_prevent_role_escalation.sql (NEW)
    - Redefines handle_new_user() a third time (schema.sql ->
      schema_phase5.sql -> schema_phase7.sql -> this file). The only
      change from schema_phase7.sql's version: the role value is now
      whitelisted in plpgsql before the insert — only 'publisher' is
      accepted as an explicit request; anything else (including 'admin',
      an unrecognised string, or no role key at all) falls back to
      'business'. Every other column this function already populated
      (full_name, company_name, phone, email_verified) is carried over
      unchanged.
    - Adds prevent_role_change(), a BEFORE UPDATE trigger function on
      public.profiles, and its trigger trg_prevent_role_change. Shape is
      deliberately identical to prevent_self_verification(): if
      auth.uid() is not null (a real client session) and the session is
      not an admin, new.role is forced back to old.role. auth.uid() is
      null for trusted server-side/service-role contexts, so the
      documented "make yourself an admin" SQL-editor UPDATE at the
      bottom of schema.sql, and any future server-side admin-promotion
      tooling, both continue to work exactly as before — only a live
      client session gets blocked.
    - No existing table, column, policy, or function is dropped or
      altered destructively; this is purely additive, matching every
      other schema_phase*.sql file's own stated convention.

- supabase/tests/prevent_role_escalation_test.sql (NEW)
    - pgTAP test file, 6 assertions, following the exact style already
      established by rls_channels_publishers_channel_requests_test.sql
      and enforce_channel_request_transition_test.sql
      (set_config('request.jwt.claim.sub', ...) + `set role authenticated`
      to simulate a real session; lives_ok + a follow-up is() for cases
      where RLS/a trigger silently no-ops a write rather than raising an
      error).
    - Covers: (1) a signUp()-style auth.users insert with
      raw_user_meta_data containing role: 'admin' produces a 'business'
      profile, not 'admin'; (2) the same signup path still correctly
      creates a 'publisher' profile when that's genuinely requested
      (regression check, so the whitelist doesn't accidentally break the
      legitimate publisher flow); (3) a logged-in business user's own
      UPDATE ... SET role = 'admin' runs without SQL error but is
      silently reverted by the trigger; (4) an admin can still change
      another user's role, confirming the trigger only blocks non-admin
      sessions and doesn't lock the column entirely.

- supabase/tests/README.md (EDITED)
    - Added a new section documenting how to run
